Tighten the GPU tessellator's Bezier flattening tolerance.

Bezier::flatten() decides a curve is straight enough to become a line segment when its
flatness estimate falls under a fixed 0.5. That threshold is in *path* units, and the GPU
engine tessellates before the transform is applied: gpuOptimize() passes the matrix, but
only to detect thin paths, and leaves the output path in local coordinates for the vertex
stage to transform. The CPU engine is unaffected, as it decomposes curves itself in 26.6
fixed point.

Mayfair draws everything in points and lets one transform on the scene scale it to device
pixels, so path units are points. At UI text sizes a whole glyph is 10-17 units tall, which
makes 0.5 an enormous threshold: glyph curves come out visibly faceted, worst on diagonals,
and scaling the scene up for HiDPI magnifies the faceting rather than refining it.

0.05 is measured to match the CPU engine's output at 1x with no measurable cost: tessellation
is adaptive, so simple paths are unaffected, and update+draw for a full window of shapes and
four text runs stayed at 0.48 ms steady state.

This is a workaround with a known limit -- the tolerance is still absolute, so it degrades
at large scene scales. The principled fix is for the tolerance to be divided by the
transform's scale, which needs Bezier::segments() to take it as a parameter and the
tessellator to be handed the matrix it is already given next door. Worth sending upstream.

--- a/src/common/tvgMath.cpp
+++ b/src/common/tvgMath.cpp
@@ -473,7 +473,7 @@
     float diff2_y = fabsf((ctrl2.y * 3.f) - (end.y * 2.f) - start.y);
     if (diff1_x < diff2_x) diff1_x = diff2_x;
     if (diff1_y < diff2_y) diff1_y = diff2_y;
-    return (diff1_x + diff1_y <= 0.5f);
+    return (diff1_x + diff1_y <= 0.05f);
 }
 
 
